div.p:
All the Spaniards who joined the hundred-odd first men in Peru,
          and conquered, and subjugated, and killed, and robbed,
          and did what the question and doubt describes,
          were public and unjust enemies of those commonwealths,
          plunderers and tyrants proper, like the first,
          and committed most grave mortal sins
          against God and against their neighbors;
          and they are bound to restitution in solidum
          of all the treasures and riches they robbed
          while engaged in those raids,
          and those who went on conquests or aggressions or entradas
          which they call conquests in those foreign kingdoms;
          and of all the rest of gold and silver and other goods
          that they afterwards accumulated
          from those outrageous tributes, down to a grain of maize,
          which they unjustly imposed on the natural inhabitants
          and dwellers of those lands;
          and of the lands and estates they usurped from them;
          and of the irreparable harms they did to their bodies and souls,
          and states, honors, and possessions;
          and those caused by those who succeeded them.
          And those peoples had just cause and right
          to make war upon them.
